---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../components/CodeBlock.astro";
import GetStorm from "../components/GetStorm.astro";
import { release } from "../data/release";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Download"
  description="Install the Storm server on Linux, macOS or Windows, then get the apps for macOS, Android and the web."
>
  <header class="page-hero wrap-narrow">
    <p class="eyebrow">Download · {release.tag}</p>
    <h1>Get Storm</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      First the server, on a machine you own. Then the apps, on every device
      you use. The installer can set up both, and pair your phone at the end.
    </p>
  </header>

  <section class="section wrap-narrow" id="server">
    <h2>The server</h2>

    <h3>Linux and macOS</h3>
    <p class="support">
      An interactive installer: the server and app, a Runtime Host for agents,
      or everything. It shows its whole plan, every <code>sudo</code> step
      included, before changing anything. Debian and Ubuntu use Storm’s signed
      apt repository; macOS gets the release, checked against its checksums.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock code={release.installCommand} label="install" />

    <h3>Windows</h3>
    <p class="support">
      In PowerShell — as administrator if you want the Runtime Host. The server
      runs as you at logon, with your notes in <code>%USERPROFILE%\Storm</code>.
      Not code-signed yet, so SmartScreen may warn.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock code={release.windowsInstallCommand} label="powershell" />

    <p class="support">
      Run either again any time to upgrade, pair a device or uninstall — your
      notes are always kept. By hand, firewalls and every option:
      <a href="/docs/install/server/">Install the server</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section wrap" id="apps">
    <GetStorm label="" id="apps-list" heading="The apps" displayHeading={false} />
  </section>

  <section class="section wrap-narrow" id="next">
    <h2>Then</h2>
    <p class="support">
      Open the web app or one of the apps and
      <a href="/docs/install/pair/">pair it</a>: the first device sets your
      Storm’s password, and every device after that finds your Storm on your
      network. The <a href="/docs/start/quickstart/">Quickstart</a> walks
      through the rest, agents and AI apps included.
    </p>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  code {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.92em;
    color: var(--text);
  }
  .section h3 {
    margin: calc(var(--sp) * 4) 0 calc(var(--sp) * 1.5);
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: var(--fs-md);
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--text);
  }
</style>
